// TASK 2: Foundations of Federation and SSO
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Why Federation Exists</h2>
    <p>Before federation, every application maintained its own list of usernames and passwords. Each new app meant another credential set for the user to remember and another database for an attacker to target. Federation removes this by designating one party as the source of truth for identity.</p>

    <p>Think of it like a passport. You do not present a different ID to every country you visit. One government issues the passport, and every other country agrees to trust it. In federation terms, that issuing government is the <strong>Identity Provider (IdP)</strong>, and each country checking it at the border is a <strong>Service Provider (SP)</strong>.</p>

    <p>The SP never sees your password. It only sees a signed statement from the IdP saying who you are. This is the core shift: authentication happens once, at the IdP, and every SP simply trusts the result.</p>

