// TASK 3: SAML 2.0 Mechanics
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Assertions as Notarized Letters</h2>
    <p>A SAML assertion is an XML document the IdP issues after authenticating a user. It contains an <strong>Issuer</strong>, a <strong>Subject</strong> identifying who logged in, a set of <strong>Conditions</strong> restricting when and where it is valid, and optionally an <strong>AttributeStatement</strong> carrying extra user data.</p>

    <p>Think of an assertion like a notarized, addressed letter. The digital signature is the notary seal proving nobody altered it. The <code>Recipient</code> field is the address on the envelope, meaning only that specific SP should accept it. The <code>NotOnOrAfter</code> field is an expiry date stamped on the letter itself.</p>

    <p>The IdP sends this assertion to the browser, which forwards it to the SP using a binding, the transport mechanism for the message.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Binding</th>
                <th>Typical Use</th>
                <th>Size Limit</th>
                <th>Signing Location</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>HTTP-POST</td>
                <td>Carrying the full assertion from IdP to SP</td>
                <td>Effectively none (form body)</td>
                <td>Signature embedded in the XML itself</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>HTTP-Redirect</td>
                <td>Sending the initial authentication request to the IdP</td>
                <td>Constrained by URL length</td>
                <td>Signature appended as a query parameter</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- SVG: SP-initiated SAML flow
