// TASK 4: OAuth 2.0 Mechanics
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Authorization, Not Authentication</h2>
    <p>OAuth 2.0 is often misunderstood as a login protocol. It is not. OAuth answers a narrower question: what is this client allowed to do on my behalf? It defines four roles: the <strong>Resource Owner</strong> (the user), the <strong>Client</strong> (the app requesting access), the <strong>Authorization Server</strong>, and the <strong>Resource Server</strong> holding the protected data.</p>

    <p>Think of an OAuth access token like a valet key. It starts the car and lets the valet drive it to the parking spot, but it cannot open the trunk or the glovebox. The token grants a limited, scoped capability, not proof of who is holding it.</p>

    <p>The most common flow today is the <strong>Authorization Code flow with PKCE</strong>. PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) prevents an attacker who intercepts the authorization code from exchanging it for a token, because only the original client holds the secret verifier needed to complete the exchange.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Grant Type</th>
                <th>Use Case</th>
                <th>Security Notes</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Authorization Code + PKCE</td>
                <td>Web, mobile, and single-page apps</td>
                <td>Current recommended standard for user-facing clients</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Client Credentials</td>
                <td>Service-to-service, no end user involved</td>
                <td>Client authenticates directly with its own secret</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Implicit (deprecated)</td>
                <td>Legacy single-page apps</td>
                <td>Tokens exposed in the URL fragment, no client authentication</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- SVG: Authorization Code + PKCE exchange
         Teaching purpose: show the learner that the authorization code and the token are exchanged in
         two separate steps, which they would otherwise misunderstand as the redirect itself handing over
         a usable access token. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
