// TASK 5: OpenID Connect
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Adding Identity on Top of Authorization</h2>
    <p>OAuth 2.0 hands the client a keycard: proof it can access certain resources. OpenID Connect (OIDC) adds a name badge on top of that keycard, a signed <strong>ID Token</strong> that actually states who the user is. OIDC is built directly on OAuth 2.0, reusing its flows and adding one new token type.</p>

    <p>The ID Token is a JWT (JSON Web Token): a compact, signed structure with a header, a payload of claims, and a signature. Unlike the access token, the ID Token is meant to be read and validated by the client itself, not just passed along to a resource server.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Claim</th>
                <th>Meaning</th>
                <th>Example Value</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>iss</code></td>
                <td>Issuer that signed the token</td>
                <td>https://idp.example.com</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>sub</code></td>
                <td>Unique identifier for the authenticated user</td>
                <td>a1b2c3d4</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>aud</code></td>
                <td>Intended recipient client of the token</td>
                <td>client_9f8e7d</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>exp</code></td>
                <td>Expiration time as a Unix timestamp</td>
                <td>1751972400</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Validate Every Time, Not Just Once</h4>
        <p>A client must check <code>iss</code> matches the expected IdP and <code>aud</code> matches its own client ID on every single ID Token it receives. Skipping either check lets a token issued for a different client or a different IdP be accepted as valid.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: SP/IdP Trust Configuration
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Establishing Trust Before Any Login Happens</h2>
    <p>An SP and an IdP do not trust each other automatically. An administrator on each side exchanges metadata: entity IDs identifying each party, the <strong>Assertion Consumer Service (ACS)</strong> URL where the SP expects to receive assertions, and the signing certificate the IdP will use.</p>

    <p>This is like two offices exchanging notarized business cards before either will accept the other's paperwork. Once exchanged, the SP knows exactly which certificate to trust and exactly which URL is allowed to receive assertions on its behalf.</p>

    <p>Key settings an engineer configures during this exchange include the required NameID format, whether assertions and responses must be signed and or encrypted, allowed clock skew for timestamp checks, and whether <strong>IdP-initiated SSO</strong> is permitted, meaning a flow that starts at the IdP rather than the SP.</p>

    <!-- SVG: metadata exchange before login
         Teaching purpose: show the learner that trust configuration happens once, ahead of time, between
         administrators, which they would otherwise misunderstand as something negotiated automatically
         during each login attempt. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
